1
00:00:27,033 --> 00:00:33,594
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is a Madhyamanikaya 82, and it's a...

2
00:00:33,914 --> 00:00:41,716
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just read out this discussion that's happening between Venerable Ratapada and a certain king at the time.

3
00:00:43,836 --> 00:00:46,417
[SPEAKER_01]: And this king describes four kinds of lusts

4
00:00:47,844 --> 00:00:54,948
[SPEAKER_01]: That a person can go through, and then on account of that, would ordain as a monk, go forth as a holy man.

5
00:00:57,989 --> 00:01:02,792
[SPEAKER_01]: But he points out that Venerable Ratpāla has not experienced those losses.

6
00:01:03,312 --> 00:01:04,913
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why has he ordained?

7
00:01:07,534 --> 00:01:15,118
[SPEAKER_01]: And he replies, I went forth because he understood these four summaries of the dhamma taught by the Buddha.

8
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:27,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when they're old, they've got nothing more to do.

9
00:01:27,842 --> 00:01:33,747
[SPEAKER_01]: They... loss of health, they get sick.

10
00:01:34,928 --> 00:01:37,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Loss of wealth and loss of relatives.

11
00:01:42,094 --> 00:01:52,983
[SPEAKER_01]: So... and... Venerable Ratapala describes these, why he went forth, on account of these four summaries of the Dhamma.

12
00:01:54,244 --> 00:01:54,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Number one is

13
00:01:55,610 --> 00:01:57,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Life in any world is unstable.

14
00:01:58,591 --> 00:02:00,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Which kind of relates to that aging, you know.

15
00:02:01,313 --> 00:02:02,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Things are aging.

16
00:02:03,434 --> 00:02:04,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Things are falling apart.

17
00:02:05,175 --> 00:02:07,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, but I mean unstable in a sense.

18
00:02:07,456 --> 00:02:08,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you don't need to...

19
00:02:09,116 --> 00:02:10,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Your old age is not guaranteed.

20
00:02:11,978 --> 00:02:12,958
[SPEAKER_00]: You may not live that long.

21
00:02:14,359 --> 00:02:18,781
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as you said so many times before, I don't know from what book that quote is, but

22
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:22,382
[SPEAKER_00]: As soon as you're born, you're old enough to die.

23
00:02:22,782 --> 00:02:25,744
[SPEAKER_00]: I think Heidegger was quoting it from somebody else, if I remember correctly.

24
00:02:26,324 --> 00:02:32,949
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, yeah, as soon as there is a manifestation, implication of death is there at any given time.

25
00:02:34,050 --> 00:02:35,371
[SPEAKER_00]: The implication of cessation.

26
00:02:36,752 --> 00:02:39,914
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether you are 10, 20, 60 years old, doesn't matter.

27
00:02:39,934 --> 00:02:40,935
[SPEAKER_00]: That implication is there.

28
00:02:42,116 --> 00:02:46,039
[SPEAKER_00]: And if somebody becomes aware of that, you realize, oh, yeah, no, that is the reality.

29
00:02:46,919 --> 00:02:48,080
[SPEAKER_00]: So, life is unstable.

30
00:02:50,038 --> 00:03:07,445
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that whole Hindu idea of four stages of life, whereby the only last stage is when you go forth and that's how you get most of the whole life, it's completely based on these assumptions that you will reach the old age and that your faculties will be working in the old age and you won't be kind of affected by illness and so on.

31
00:03:08,006 --> 00:03:18,010
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just basically a worldly idea of trying to squeeze as much as they can out of sensuality and then go and practice the holy life, failing to understand that the longer you stay

32
00:03:18,642 --> 00:03:23,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Not practicing the holy life, the more of a burden you accumulate.

33
00:03:24,125 --> 00:03:47,477
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you were not able to go against the grain and give up the burden when there wasn't so much burden accumulated, you're not going to be able to do it when you were 60 with, like, you know, a lot of burden in your mind of... and I mean burden in terms of, like, you know, attachments, habits, views, assumptions, control, sense of mastery,

34
00:03:47,954 --> 00:03:49,756
[SPEAKER_00]: sense of mind, all of that proliferates.

35
00:03:49,796 --> 00:03:50,876
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a static thing.

36
00:03:51,217 --> 00:03:52,178
[SPEAKER_00]: The habits.

37
00:03:52,278 --> 00:03:53,038
[SPEAKER_00]: The habits, yeah.

38
00:03:53,619 --> 00:03:54,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Ferment, proliferate.

39
00:03:54,860 --> 00:04:01,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, even these small mundane habits are quite a lot for people, but let alone, you know, the ones that your kind of life depends on.

40
00:04:01,785 --> 00:04:11,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, understanding that life is unstable, in itself, is more than enough to try and do something about it, if you truly understand the way

41
00:04:19,567 --> 00:04:21,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Aging is not when you turn 60.

42
00:04:21,388 --> 00:04:22,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Aging is already this.

43
00:04:23,969 --> 00:04:30,891
[SPEAKER_00]: And so you are already aging, and you are already heading towards that disintegration of the aggregates.

44
00:04:31,271 --> 00:04:38,194
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not guaranteed that you're gonna see the, you know, 80, 90 year old age or something.

45
00:04:40,775 --> 00:04:43,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Because manifestation is there, means possibility of death is there.

46
00:04:43,876 --> 00:04:45,157
[SPEAKER_00]: But it feels like I am.

47
00:04:45,697 --> 00:04:46,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

48
00:04:50,269 --> 00:04:54,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of discernment, it's a very perilous game.

49
00:04:55,591 --> 00:04:55,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

50
00:04:55,911 --> 00:04:59,853
[SPEAKER_01]: So, putting off these things that you... you know... You surely do know.

51
00:04:59,973 --> 00:05:01,454
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to sort this out.

52
00:05:01,534 --> 00:05:02,494
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll do it later.

53
00:05:02,794 --> 00:05:03,674
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll do it later.

54
00:05:03,694 --> 00:05:04,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me get the most out of this.

55
00:05:05,695 --> 00:05:05,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

56
00:05:06,175 --> 00:05:09,457
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... well, that's... that's smarter talking.

57
00:05:12,718 --> 00:05:15,719
[SPEAKER_01]: And this second summary of the dhamma,

58
00:05:17,979 --> 00:05:22,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Life in any world has no shelter and no protector.

59
00:05:22,701 --> 00:05:24,162
[SPEAKER_01]: That seems to relate to the illness.

60
00:05:24,982 --> 00:05:25,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

61
00:05:25,963 --> 00:05:26,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, safety.

62
00:05:27,383 --> 00:05:36,067
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like, you know, when Buddha compares it like you should not allow your consciousness to rest upon anything as a safe space, safe place.

63
00:05:36,087 --> 00:05:36,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it isn't.

64
00:05:37,708 --> 00:05:43,630
[SPEAKER_00]: The only safe place is complete cessation and dispassion and abandoning of everything, this life, everything, everything.

65
00:05:44,851 --> 00:05:45,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Humanity

66
00:05:46,153 --> 00:05:50,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Love, attachments, things that are dear to you, all of that is a trap.

67
00:05:51,656 --> 00:05:54,277
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what the Buddha was actually teaching.

68
00:05:54,797 --> 00:05:59,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Very different from what people think the Buddha was teaching, you know, world peace and loving-kindness and so on.

69
00:06:00,060 --> 00:06:01,161
[SPEAKER_00]: The one mind is safe.

70
00:06:01,621 --> 00:06:03,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, nothing, nothing is safe.

71
00:06:03,402 --> 00:06:09,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, compared, what is it, compared consciousness to the whip, like being hit with a whip.

72
00:06:10,345 --> 00:06:10,945
[SPEAKER_00]: With the spears.

73
00:06:11,086 --> 00:06:12,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, with the spears, yeah, the spears, yeah.

74
00:06:12,967 --> 00:06:13,847
[SPEAKER_00]: The contact was a whip.

75
00:06:15,141 --> 00:06:24,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Similar to where... yeah, so each time your consciousness gets established upon something, you should regard it as being struck with a spear, because that's the peril of it.

76
00:06:24,986 --> 00:06:33,450
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's truly nothing to regard as suitable to rest upon.

77
00:06:37,512 --> 00:06:38,293
[SPEAKER_01]: No safety.

78
00:06:38,553 --> 00:06:39,093
[SPEAKER_00]: No safety.

79
00:06:39,494 --> 00:06:41,555
[SPEAKER_00]: The only safety is that cessation of ownership.

80
00:06:42,715 --> 00:06:44,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Complete removal of this passion,

81
00:06:45,495 --> 00:06:50,599
[SPEAKER_00]: And pretty much any appreciation of life or anything within it, it needs to be abandoned.

82
00:06:52,080 --> 00:06:56,483
[SPEAKER_00]: So, disenchantment, revulsion, all those things the Buddha was describing.

83
00:06:57,084 --> 00:06:58,885
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can, you can, yeah, try and be...

84
00:06:58,905 --> 00:07:05,190
[SPEAKER_00]: So that the mind turns away, and completely lets go of any need for safety.

85
00:07:08,572 --> 00:07:10,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Because need for safety is based on the wrong assumptions.

86
00:07:11,254 --> 00:07:12,635
[SPEAKER_00]: It's based on the sense of ownership,

87
00:07:13,070 --> 00:07:13,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense of I am.

88
00:07:13,650 --> 00:07:14,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense of mine.

89
00:07:14,730 --> 00:07:16,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, the need to protect what's mine.

90
00:07:17,731 --> 00:07:22,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Need to find a safe space for what's mine, where it will be safe.

91
00:07:22,972 --> 00:07:24,412
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

92
00:07:24,492 --> 00:07:31,314
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for as long as you're seeking some safety, you're implying some ownership of something you want to be safe.

93
00:07:32,674 --> 00:07:36,215
[SPEAKER_00]: And thus, you won't be safe because of that very thing.

94
00:07:36,235 --> 00:07:37,195
[SPEAKER_00]: That vulnerability.

95
00:07:37,295 --> 00:07:37,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

96
00:07:40,525 --> 00:07:47,747
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are vulnerable because you take things as... Well, you take things that are not yours, as yours.

97
00:07:48,948 --> 00:07:49,968
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're in debt.

98
00:07:50,268 --> 00:07:50,908
[SPEAKER_00]: You're vulnerable.

99
00:07:52,689 --> 00:07:59,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Māra has access to you.

100
00:07:59,471 --> 00:08:01,612
[SPEAKER_01]: The third summary.

101
00:08:01,732 --> 00:08:04,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Life in any world has nothing of its own.

102
00:08:05,093 --> 00:08:07,194
[SPEAKER_01]: One has to leave all and pass on.

103
00:08:12,303 --> 00:08:16,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Have you ever gained it, really?

104
00:08:16,646 --> 00:08:17,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

105
00:08:17,347 --> 00:08:18,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Have you ever secured it?

106
00:08:18,888 --> 00:08:21,430
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, like, again, even like, oh, this is mine...

107
00:08:21,450 --> 00:08:27,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Remember, I think we spoke about it a while ago, in one of the talks, like, even the notion of things that belong to you.

108
00:08:27,314 --> 00:08:30,717
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to start, you know, breaking them with a hammer to prove that you're not the owner.

109
00:08:30,737 --> 00:08:42,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Just start... just make the effort and understand what do you mean, like, for yourself, when you say, oh, this item, this is mine, this belongs to me.

110
00:08:42,720 --> 00:08:44,081
[SPEAKER_00]: What does that belonging entail?

111
00:08:44,801 --> 00:08:46,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, how does it feel in a way?

112
00:08:47,043 --> 00:08:48,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what is it as a phenomenon?

113
00:08:49,424 --> 00:08:52,466
[SPEAKER_00]: That belonging of something to you?

114
00:08:55,127 --> 00:08:56,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that mean you're in its control?

115
00:08:58,109 --> 00:08:59,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that mean you're its creator?

116
00:08:59,410 --> 00:09:01,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Does that mean you... it cannot be taken away?

117
00:09:02,251 --> 00:09:03,692
[SPEAKER_00]: What does that ownership imply?

118
00:09:05,593 --> 00:09:08,615
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize that that's the trick of the ownership is

119
00:09:09,633 --> 00:09:12,694
[SPEAKER_00]: The power of the ownership is that people don't think about it.

120
00:09:12,734 --> 00:09:16,135
[SPEAKER_00]: You just kind of skate on top of it, take it for granted.

121
00:09:16,195 --> 00:09:17,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, mine, mine, mine, mine, mine.

122
00:09:18,136 --> 00:09:25,998
[SPEAKER_00]: But even without any Buddhist practice, if a mind would just stop and think about it, like, what does it mean when something is mine?

123
00:09:26,499 --> 00:09:27,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, how would I describe that?

124
00:09:28,459 --> 00:09:30,960
[SPEAKER_00]: What are the properties of that?

125
00:09:30,980 --> 00:09:32,561
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize the contradiction in terms.

126
00:09:33,241 --> 00:09:36,442
[SPEAKER_00]: And certainly, it would help you not take

127
00:09:37,520 --> 00:09:39,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that are yours, so much is yours.

128
00:09:41,143 --> 00:09:43,225
[SPEAKER_00]: They wouldn't be as much yours, let's say like that.

129
00:09:43,786 --> 00:09:48,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you realize, well, yeah, the notion of mine, it's quite relative.

130
00:09:48,510 --> 00:09:53,234
[SPEAKER_00]: It's mine for as long as that thing is there, but I have no fundamental say in that.

131
00:09:53,754 --> 00:09:57,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Or it's mine for as long as I'm there, but I have no fundamental say in that.

132
00:09:59,399 --> 00:10:01,501
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, okay, it's relatively mine.

133
00:10:02,162 --> 00:10:04,084
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, I'm the current user of it.

134
00:10:05,467 --> 00:10:07,189
[SPEAKER_00]: But people don't.

135
00:10:07,309 --> 00:10:14,915
[SPEAKER_00]: But by not thinking about that, what does mine mean, they do take it in that kind of infinite sense.

136
00:10:15,935 --> 00:10:21,039
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why always that infinite threat of when something mine is threatened to be taken away.

137
00:10:21,480 --> 00:10:22,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you stop and think about it.

138
00:10:22,601 --> 00:10:23,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, I don't care much about it.

139
00:10:23,922 --> 00:10:25,743
[SPEAKER_00]: But I care that mine is attacked.

140
00:10:26,323 --> 00:10:27,744
[SPEAKER_00]: That mine is under a threat.

141
00:10:28,465 --> 00:10:30,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I never questioned the notion of mine.

142
00:10:31,452 --> 00:10:37,817
[SPEAKER_00]: And I take it in an infinite sense, ultimate sense, that mine means mine forever and must stay and be protected that way.

143
00:10:39,438 --> 00:10:44,162
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's even like, again, even further contradiction.

144
00:10:44,942 --> 00:10:47,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it were yours, you wouldn't need to protect it.

145
00:10:48,265 --> 00:10:53,289
[SPEAKER_00]: If it were in your control, it would not be under any threat.

146
00:10:53,429 --> 00:10:54,830
[SPEAKER_00]: It could not be under a threat.

147
00:10:56,131 --> 00:11:00,354
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were the master of its existence, it would listen only to you.

148
00:11:01,365 --> 00:11:02,245
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of that's the case.

149
00:11:02,646 --> 00:11:07,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, people go around with the attitude, mind belongs to me.

150
00:11:10,068 --> 00:11:20,532
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you can only... that's the way, you know, that's where passion is, you know, you can't have passion for something if it's not really yours.

151
00:11:20,552 --> 00:11:21,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

152
00:11:21,473 --> 00:11:22,893
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't really matter, it's gone.

153
00:11:24,094 --> 00:11:27,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's, yeah, if you start questioning that notion of mind,

154
00:11:28,170 --> 00:11:35,072
[SPEAKER_00]: You are already narrowing down, I suppose you could say, the basis for proliferation of passion.

155
00:11:36,153 --> 00:11:38,894
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are diminishing passion, in simple terms.

156
00:11:40,214 --> 00:11:42,995
[SPEAKER_00]: By questioning the notion of mind, you are diminishing passion.

157
00:11:43,035 --> 00:11:50,818
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will be unlikely to be trying to get things.

158
00:11:51,238 --> 00:11:51,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

159
00:11:51,838 --> 00:11:52,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

160
00:11:52,278 --> 00:11:52,859
[SPEAKER_00]: Or not as much.

161
00:11:52,879 --> 00:11:55,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Striving really hard to get things.

162
00:11:55,599 --> 00:11:56,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

163
00:11:56,120 --> 00:11:56,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Not be...

164
00:11:58,839 --> 00:12:05,262
[SPEAKER_00]: As gripped by that kind of blind ambition, without any context of its ultimate relativity.

165
00:12:05,662 --> 00:12:11,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, and relativity, I mean, exactly, yeah, yeah, I work for it, I earned it, it's mine, I built it, I created it.

166
00:12:11,866 --> 00:12:15,288
[SPEAKER_00]: That still doesn't mean that it cannot be taken away and that it won't be taken away.

167
00:12:15,748 --> 00:12:22,552
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what Venerable Rūpa Pāla says is, fundamentally, from that ultimate point of view of, you know, life and death, pretty much,

168
00:12:29,483 --> 00:12:31,545
[SPEAKER_01]: You're able to re-evaluate your investments.

169
00:12:31,645 --> 00:12:32,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

170
00:12:32,366 --> 00:12:32,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

171
00:12:32,826 --> 00:12:40,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not acting out of this assumption that you will live forever and that your possessions will carry on even after you die or something, my legacy and stuff like that.

172
00:12:40,653 --> 00:12:41,874
[SPEAKER_00]: That becomes inconceivable.

173
00:12:43,415 --> 00:12:50,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Certainly inconceivable as a motivator for action and, you know, and provider of ambition.

174
00:12:50,221 --> 00:12:56,086
[SPEAKER_00]: That won't be possible because ambition and action of that kind, it basically always has to be rooted in passion.

175
00:12:58,045 --> 00:12:59,506
[SPEAKER_01]: It makes things easier, doesn't it?

176
00:13:00,206 --> 00:13:01,486
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm only here for a little while.

177
00:13:02,807 --> 00:13:05,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I need to have all these requisites?

178
00:13:06,889 --> 00:13:12,131
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, do I have to have the most exquisite house?

179
00:13:12,171 --> 00:13:16,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, if the context is sustained, then life becomes easier.

180
00:13:16,893 --> 00:13:18,934
[SPEAKER_00]: But sustaining the context is what's not easy.

181
00:13:19,794 --> 00:13:21,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Or building it up, that's what's not easy.

182
00:13:22,915 --> 00:13:24,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's not natural.

183
00:13:25,336 --> 00:13:26,977
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not natural to go against the grain.

184
00:13:28,540 --> 00:13:38,442
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you invest the effort to develop wisdom in that context, then, yeah, life will be much easier.

185
00:13:38,722 --> 00:13:38,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

186
00:13:39,002 --> 00:13:42,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you will be less able to suffer.

187
00:13:43,043 --> 00:13:47,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't have so much passion in your mind that binds you to things gratuitously.

188
00:13:48,324 --> 00:13:50,025
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when they change, you will not be affected.

189
00:13:50,245 --> 00:13:51,385
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the definition of peace.

190
00:14:04,444 --> 00:14:12,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind doesn't know anything other than the ownership of the senses, of the body, and everything else that has proliferated through that.

191
00:14:13,489 --> 00:14:20,013
[SPEAKER_00]: My house, my family, my friend, my reputation, my name, my business, my savings, my this, my this, my this, my this, my all this.

192
00:14:20,033 --> 00:14:20,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Burdened by it.

